    "description": "Not every mold problem is a basement. A laundry room where clothes hang to dry, a finished lower-level bedroom that smells musty every August, or a spare room on a north wall all need drying, but a 50-pint basement machine is more than they need. The Cube 35 is the size we would put there. The ENERGY STAR certified list shows the current -A revision at 35.82 pints per day and 378 kWh a year, with the same 2.01 L/kWh efficiency as the 50-pint Cubes. Its party trick is the tank: Midea's manual gives four fill levels from about 2.25 to 4.25 gallons, several times a normal bucket, so a unit holding a laundry room at 50% can often go a day or more between emptying. A gravity hose connection and a bucketless mode cover rooms with a nearby drain. Midea lists auto defrost and auto restart, Wi-Fi control through its SmartHome app, and Set, Max and Continuous modes. It measures about 14.45 by 14.76 by 14.57 inches nested and weighs 39.6 pounds. It usually sells for about $240 to $300 on Amazon."
