      "Continuous drain hose option on the drain-hose version"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Rated for spaces only up to about 270 square feet",
      "Listed at 470 watts on high, so it costs more per pint than a compressor unit in a warm room"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a mildewed closet, a cold storage room or an RV, where a full-size compressor dehumidifier is too big or too cold to work well.",
    "description": "Some of the most stubborn mildew grows in small, cold places: a closet on an exterior wall, an unheated storage room, a cabin or RV closed up for the winter. A compressor dehumidifier is the wrong tool for those, because it is oversized for the space and its coil frosts in the cold. The Ivation IVADDH06 uses a desiccant wheel instead, which pulls moisture out with an absorbent material rather than a chilled coil, so its output depends far less on room temperature. Retailers list it at 13 pints per day at 68°F and 60% relative humidity, for spaces up to about 270 square feet, with an operating range starting at 33°F. It weighs about 14 pounds and collects water in a small removable tank, and the drain-hose version adds continuous drainage, which we would choose for any space you do not check daily. The trade-off is power: listings give 470 watts on high and 280 on low, so in a warm room it uses more electricity per pint than a compressor unit. In a cold closet or garage room, that is the price of a machine that keeps working. It usually sells for about $230 to $290."
