        <div id="mold-basics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Dehumidifier Can and Cannot Do About Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold needs three things to grow: moisture, something to eat and a temperature people find comfortable. In a basement you cannot remove the food, because wood framing, the paper face of drywall, cardboard boxes and ordinary house dust all qualify, and you cannot make the room too cold for mold without making it useless. That leaves moisture. Mold spores are present in essentially every home, indoors and out, and they only become a colony where a surface stays damp long enough for them to germinate. A dehumidifier attacks that one lever by pulling water vapor out of the air, which lowers relative humidity and, with it, the moisture available on walls, joists and stored belongings.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a dehumidifier does not do is kill mold. Drying an existing colony makes it go dormant, but dormant mold still stains, still releases spores and allergens when disturbed, and wakes up the next time humidity climbs. So the honest answer to anyone searching for a dehumidifier to get rid of mold is that it gets rid of the conditions, not the mold. Visible growth has to be cleaned or, on porous materials, cut out, and our <a href="/best-mold-remover/">best mold remover guide</a> covers the products that do that job.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier also cannot keep up with liquid water. If a foundation wall weeps after every storm, a pipe sweats onto the floor, or a bathroom has no working exhaust fan, the machine is mopping a tap that is still running. Fix the source first, then clean, then dry. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">getting rid of mold in a basement</a> walks through that order step by step, and it is the order that keeps mold from coming back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where a dehumidifier is the right tool, and often the only practical one, is holding a basement, crawl space or laundry room below the mold threshold month after month. Cool basement air turns humid every summer even in a house with no leaks, because warm outdoor air drifts in and cools. That job is less about the biggest pint rating and more about a machine that runs for months without anyone tending it, which is why every pick in this guide was chosen for drainage, restart behavior or cold-room performance before raw capacity. For the general sizes and brands, start with our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifiers for the home</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A dehumidifier prevents mold. It does not remove mold that is already there.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Clean or remove existing growth, fix the water source, then run a dehumidifier to keep relative humidity at about 50% so it never comes back.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="target-humidity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Humidity Number That Stops Mold Growth
          </h2>

          <p>
            The EPA's guidance is to keep indoor relative humidity below 60%, and ideally between 30% and 50%. ENERGY STAR's dehumidifier advice uses the same 30% to 50% range and warns that anything above it may promote mold growth. For a basement, 50% is the practical target: low enough to stop growth with a margin, high enough that the dehumidifier is not running around the clock chasing a number the space cannot hold. Our article on <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> goes into the seasonal detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why aim for 50 rather than just under 60? Because the number on the display is the humidity of the air passing the sensor, and mold grows on surfaces, which are often colder than the air. Relative humidity rises as air cools. Air at 70°F and 50% relative humidity that touches a 55°F foundation wall is at roughly 84% relative humidity right at the wall surface, comfortably in the range mold likes. The same air at 60% reaches its dew point on that wall and condenses. That is how a basement grows mold along the bottom of exterior walls and behind boxes while the dehumidifier display reads a reassuring 55%.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in humidistats are not precision instruments, and owners commonly report readings several points away from a separate hygrometer. A small digital hygrometer costs little and is the most useful accessory you can buy. Put it in the far corner or beside the wall where mold has appeared before, not next to the dehumidifier, and adjust the setpoint until that hygrometer reads about 50%. In a large or partitioned basement, two hygrometers tell you whether one unit is actually reaching every room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed matters after any water event. The EPA advises drying water-damaged materials within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold growth. After a leak, a burst hose or a minor flood, run the dehumidifier in continuous mode rather than on the humidistat, add fans to move air across wet surfaces, and only return to a 50% setpoint once carpets, drywall and framing are dry to the touch and the hygrometer has settled.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">THE NUMBERS THAT MATTER</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white"><60%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA indoor humidity ceiling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Practical basement target</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-48 hrs</p>
