        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Size a Dehumidifier for a Mold-Prone Space
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size by how damp the space is, not just by its square footage. Since 2019 the pint rating comes from a Department of Energy test run at 65°F, which is why the largest portables are now sold as 50 pints rather than 70. The square footage on the box is a marketing estimate rather than part of that test, and it assumes a moderately damp space. A basement that already shows mold is, by definition, not moderately damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use what you can see and smell. A musty odor with no visible damp suggests a moderately damp space. Visible mold spots, condensation on cold water pipes, damp patches at the base of walls or a white mineral bloom on concrete suggest a very damp one. Standing water or active seepage means the space is wet, and no portable will fix that until the water is dealt with.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you see</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Dampness</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to buy</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Musty smell only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35 pints up to about 1,500 sq ft; 50 pints above that</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mold spots, sweating pipes, damp wall bases</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very damp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 pints per 1,000 to 1,500 sq ft, with a pump or hose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Puddles or seepage after rain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fix the water first, then 50 pints or a crawl space unit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mildewed closet or small storage room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Localized</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A small desiccant unit such as the Ivation</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            When in doubt, size up. A larger unit reaches 50% faster and then cycles off, and its energy use per pint removed is about the same as or better than a smaller unit's, because the 50-pint class has a higher efficiency requirement under ENERGY STAR. The failure mode in mold work is undersizing: a small unit runs continuously, never reaches its setpoint, and the walls stay damp. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">what size dehumidifier a basement needs</a> has worked examples.
          </p>

          <p>
            Layout matters as much as size. A dehumidifier dries the air that reaches it, so one unit in the open part of a basement may barely touch a closed storage room or a finished bedroom behind a door. Leave interior doors open, add a second smaller unit in the isolated room, or, in a crawl space, use a ducted unit that pulls air from the far end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Continuous Drainage: The Feature That Matters Most for Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier with a full bucket is a box that does nothing. The moment the bucket switch trips, the compressor stops, and in a damp basement humidity can climb back above 60% within hours. If the bucket fills at two in the morning on a Friday and nobody looks until Sunday, the space spends most of a weekend in mold-growing conditions. Mold growth depends on how many hours a surface stays damp, so an unattended unit that keeps stopping does far less good than its pint rating suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buckets on 50-pint portables hold roughly 15 to 22 pints, and a unit pulling 25 or 30 pints a day from a damp basement fills one in well under a day. There are three ways around it. A gravity hose to a floor drain or sump pit is the simplest and has nothing to fail. A built-in pump, like those in the Frigidaire, Midea Cube and AlorAir picks, lifts the water to a laundry sink or out a window. Or an external condensate pump can be added to any unit with a hose port. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">dehumidifiers with a pump guide</a> compares lift and hose routing, and our article on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> covers the workarounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pumps are the part of a portable most likely to fail, so we favored models that keep a backup. The Frigidaire FHDP5033Y1 keeps a gravity port alongside its pump, the AlorAir ships with both a pump hose and a gravity hose, and the Midea Cube's oversized tank buys a day or more of drying if the pump has to come out for cleaning. A pump that dies quietly on a unit with no fallback leaves you right back at the full-bucket problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Route hoses carefully. A gravity hose has to slope downhill the whole way, with no loops above the outlet, or water backs up into the bucket. And clean the bucket and hose every few weeks: standing condensate grows slime and can smell musty itself, which is an irritating way to discover that your mold-prevention machine needs its own mold prevention.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="auto-restart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Auto Restart, Power Cuts and Units Nobody Watches
          </h2>

          <p>
            Summer thunderstorms knock out power at exactly the time of year when basements are most humid. A dehumidifier without auto restart comes back from an outage switched off, and it stays off until someone notices. In a basement you visit once a week, that can be several days of uncontrolled humidity. Auto restart, which some manufacturers call memory starting, brings the unit back on with its previous settings as soon as power returns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among our picks, retailer spec listings confirm auto restart on the Frigidaire FHDP5033Y1, Midea lists it for the Cube line, and AlorAir lists memory starting on the HD35P. Hisense's spec sheet for the AHD5024K1W does not list it, so treat that unit as one to check after any outage, or pair it with a smart plug that reports power status. Whichever unit you buy, look for the phrase in the manual rather than assuming it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wi-Fi is useful here, not as a gadget but as a monitor. The Frigidaire and Midea apps show whether the unit is running and what humidity it reads, so you can confirm from upstairs or from work that it came back after a storm. A Wi-Fi hygrometer does the same job for any dehumidifier and gives you an independent reading at the same time. Our <a href="/best-smart-dehumidifier/">best smart dehumidifier guide</a> covers which apps are worth having.
          </p>

          <p>
            Duty cycle is the other half of unattended reliability. A basement dehumidifier in a humid climate can run well over a thousand hours a season, which is a hard life for an appliance designed for a damp bedroom. Warranty length is a reasonable proxy for how a maker expects its unit to hold up: one year is typical for portables, Hisense offers two, AlorAir advertises six with the first two full, and Santa Fe offers six on the Compact70.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-spaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cold Basements and Crawl Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compressor dehumidifiers work by chilling a coil below the dew point of the air, and as the room gets colder the coil gets closer to freezing. Below the mid 60s Fahrenheit, frost starts to form, the unit spends more time in defrost, and output falls. Most mainstream portables are rated to operate down to about 41°F. Among our picks, Hisense rates the AHD5024K1W to 38°F, AlorAir rates the HD35P down to 33.8°F, and Santa Fe lists a 49°F minimum for the Compact70.
          </p>

          <p>
            This matters for mold because the riskiest season in many basements is not midsummer but the shoulder months, when the space sits in the 50s and the air is still damp. A portable at 55°F still works, but expect noticeably less than its rated capacity, and size up if your basement stays cool. In midwinter the moisture load is usually low enough that output does not matter much, which is why a cold-rated unit matters most in spring and fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            For spaces that stay below about 50°F, two kinds of machine do better. A crawl space unit such as the AlorAir is designed for low-temperature operation from the start, with aggressive defrost and a coil sized for cold air. A desiccant unit such as the Ivation does not rely on a chilled coil at all, so it keeps drying near freezing, though it uses more electricity per pint than a compressor unit when the room is warm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces deserve their own warning. A vented, dirt-floored crawl space in a humid climate pulls in summer air that condenses on cool joists, and no dehumidifier can dry the outdoors. The space needs a vapor barrier and closed vents before a dehumidifier makes sense, and then it needs a unit built for it rather than a portable left on the ground. Our <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">best crawl space dehumidifier guide</a> covers the larger units.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mildew-smell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Mildew Smell, Laundry Rooms and Bathrooms
          </h2>

          <p>
            That musty, earthy smell people describe as mildew is caused by gases that actively growing mold and mildew release. If you can smell it, something is growing somewhere, most often behind stored boxes, under a carpet pad, inside a closet on an outside wall or in the cavity behind a leaky fixture. Air fresheners hide it; drying the space removes the cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier is usually the right first move for a smell with no visible source. As humidity falls toward 50%, growth slows and the odor typically fades over days to weeks. If the smell lingers or returns as soon as the unit is off, the source is staying wet from something other than humid air, and you need to find it. Our article on whether <a href="/will-a-dehumidifier-get-rid-of-musty-basement-smell/">a dehumidifier will get rid of a musty basement smell</a> covers where to look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laundry rooms are a common mildew spot, especially where clothes hang to dry or the dryer vent leaks. A 35-pint unit like the Midea Cube 35 is the right size for most of them, and its large tank means you are not emptying a bucket twice a day. Check the dryer vent connection while you are there, because a disconnected vent can pour a gallon or more of water vapor into the room per load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bathrooms are different. An exhaust fan vented outdoors, run during the shower and for a good while after, does more for a bathroom than any dehumidifier, and it removes the moisture at the source rather than spreading it around the house first. A small dehumidifier helps in a bathroom without a fan or one with no window, and our <a href="/best-bathroom-dehumidifier/">best bathroom dehumidifier guide</a> covers compact options. For a mildewed closet, a small desiccant unit like the Ivation is the practical tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="existing-mold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When Mold Is Already There
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you can already see mold, the sequence is fix, clean, dry, then prevent. Fix the moisture source. Clean or remove the growth. Dry the space thoroughly. Then run the dehumidifier at about 50% to keep it from coming back. Running the dehumidifier early is fine, because it helps materials dry, but it does not replace cleaning, and dormant mold left in place will regrow the next time humidity rises.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size the job before you start. EPA guidance is that an area of more than about 10 square feet, roughly a three-foot by three-foot patch, deserves more care than a quick wipe, and that porous materials with growth, such as drywall, ceiling tile and carpet, usually need to be removed rather than cleaned. Mold inside walls or ductwork, or growth after sewage or contaminated flooding, is professional territory. Our <a href="/mold-remediation-cost/">mold remediation cost guide</a> explains what a professional job involves and what it typically costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Searches for a dehumidifier for black mold are common, and the answer is the same as for any other color. Color does not tell you the species or how hazardous it is, so treat all visible growth the same way. The mold people mean by toxic black mold, Stachybotrys, needs material that stays very wet for a long time, which makes humidity control a particularly effective defense against it: a basement held at 50% simply does not stay wet enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dehumidifiers move a lot of air but are not filters; most have a coarse mesh screen meant to protect the coil, not catch spores. During and after cleanup, a HEPA air purifier captures airborne spores that disturbed mold releases, and our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">best air purifier for mold guide</a> covers sizing. Skip ozone generators and UV gadgets marketed as mold killers; our article on whether <a href="/do-ozone-generators-kill-mold/">ozone generators kill mold</a> explains why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Setup, Placement and Maintenance for Mold Control
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put the dehumidifier where air can circulate freely, usually toward the middle of the space rather than jammed into a corner, with clearance on the intake and exhaust sides as the manual specifies. Keep windows closed while it runs, and if the basement opens to the rest of the house, decide whether you are drying just the basement or the whole lower level and set the door accordingly. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-dehumidifier-in-a-basement/">where to place a dehumidifier in a basement</a> has layouts for open and partitioned spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold grows where air stalls, so help the air reach the walls. Pull stored boxes and furniture a few inches away from exterior walls, swap cardboard boxes on the slab for plastic bins on shelves, and lift anything stored directly on concrete. A small fan aimed along a problem wall can make a bigger difference than a larger dehumidifier, because it moves dried air across the coldest surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is simple and easy to forget. Rinse the air filter every few weeks during heavy use, wash the bucket with mild detergent, and check hoses for slime or kinks. Once a season, unplug the unit and vacuum dust from the coil fins. A clogged filter or coil cuts airflow, the unit runs longer to do the same work, and in a cold room it frosts sooner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run it from the spring thaw through the end of the humid season, and in many climates year-round. A heated basement may stay below 50% on its own in midwinter, but check the hygrometer before switching off rather than assuming; our article on whether <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/">you need a dehumidifier in winter</a> explains when it is safe to stop. If you are still choosing between the general-purpose units, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifier guide</a> compares the wider field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the dehumidifier to kill the mold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drying mold makes it dormant, not dead. Dormant growth still stains, still releases spores when disturbed and regrows as soon as humidity returns. Clean or remove visible mold first, fix whatever let the space get wet, and then use the dehumidifier to keep relative humidity at about 50% so it stays gone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the bucket in a damp basement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50-pint unit in a damp basement can fill its bucket in well under a day, and once it is full the compressor stops and humidity climbs straight back. For mold prevention, set up a gravity hose to a floor drain or buy a model with a built-in pump so the unit never stops on its own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the display instead of a hygrometer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Built-in humidistats commonly read several points off, and they measure the air at the unit, not the cold wall where mold grows. Put an independent hygrometer near the problem wall and adjust the setpoint until it reads about 50%. Aiming for just under 60% leaves cold surfaces damp enough for growth.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving a portable in a cold crawl space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most portables are rated only to about 41°F and lose much of their output well before that, and a damp crawl space is a hard place for a plastic appliance. Seal the crawl space first, then install a unit designed for it, with low-temperature operation, defrost and a pump or drain line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the water that is getting in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier removes water vapor; it cannot keep up with seepage, a sweating pipe or a dryer vent blowing into the room. If the unit runs constantly and the walls stay damp, find and fix the liquid water. Gutters, grading, a cracked foundation or a disconnected vent are the usual culprits.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a dehumidifier get rid of mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A dehumidifier lowers humidity so mold cannot grow, and it makes existing mold go dormant, but it does not kill or remove it. Clean small areas of visible growth, remove moldy porous materials such as drywall and carpet, fix the moisture source, and then run the dehumidifier at about 50% to keep mold from returning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should I keep to prevent mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity below 60%, ideally between 30% and 50%. In a basement, aim for about 50% measured by a separate hygrometer near the coldest wall. Cold surfaces have higher humidity than the room air, so a setting just under 60% can still leave walls damp enough for mold to grow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier help with black mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps prevent it, not remove it. The mold usually called toxic black mold needs material that stays very wet for a long time, so holding a space at 50% is an effective defense. Existing black mold should be treated like any other visible growth: cleaned or removed, with professional help for areas larger than about 10 square feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dehumidifier get rid of a mildew smell?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. The musty smell comes from actively growing mold and mildew, and as humidity drops toward 50% that growth slows and the odor typically fades over days to weeks. If the smell lingers or returns when the unit is off, something is staying wet for another reason, such as a leak or condensation, and needs fixing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for mold in a basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a basement with visible mold spots, sweating pipes or damp wall bases, plan on a 50-pint unit for every 1,000 to 1,500 square feet. A basement that only smells musty can usually manage with a 35-pint unit up to about 1,500 square feet. If in doubt, size up; an undersized unit runs nonstop without reaching its target.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I run a dehumidifier all the time to prevent mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Leave it plugged in and set to about 50% rather than on continuous mode. The humidistat runs the compressor only when needed, which saves electricity and still keeps conditions below the mold threshold. Use continuous mode only after a leak or flood, when you want maximum drying for a few days, then switch back to the setpoint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dehumidifier or an air purifier better for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do different jobs. A dehumidifier stops mold growing by removing moisture; a HEPA air purifier captures spores already in the air but does nothing about the damp that lets mold grow. For mold prevention, start with the dehumidifier. Add a HEPA purifier during and after cleanup, or if someone in the house has mold allergies.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

